Integration & APIs (Salesforce)

This section covers how Salesforce communicates with external systems and handles large-scale data exchange.

6.1 APIs

Salesforce exposes multiple APIs depending on the use case.

REST API
Uses standard HTTP methods: GET, POST, PATCH, DELETE
Lightweight and widely used
Data format: JSON
Key Features
Easy integration with web/mobile apps
Supports CRUD operations
Stateless communication
Example
GET /services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Account/
Use Cases
Web applications
Mobile apps
Real-time integrations
SOAP API
Uses XML-based messaging protocol
Requires WSDL (Web Service Definition Language)
Key Features
Strong typing (strict schema)
Built-in error handling
Enterprise-grade integrations
Use Cases
Legacy systems
Enterprise middleware (like SAP, Oracle)
Bulk API
Designed for large data volumes (millions of records)
Types
Bulk API 1.0
Bulk API 2.0 (recommended)
Key Features
Asynchronous processing
Handles batch uploads/downloads
Optimized for performance
Use Cases
Data migration
Nightly batch jobs
ETL tools
6.2 Callouts

Salesforce can call external systems via Apex.

HTTP Callouts in Apex
Used to send HTTP requests to external services
Example
Http http = new Http();
HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();

req.setEndpoint('https://api.example.com/data');
req.setMethod('GET');

HttpResponse res = http.send(req);

System.debug(res.getBody());
Important Points
Must be asynchronous in triggers (Future, Queueable)
Requires Remote Site Settings or Named Credentials
JSON Parsing
Deserialize JSON → Apex Object
String jsonString = '{"name":"Raj","age":25}';
Map<String, Object> result = (Map<String, Object>) JSON.deserializeUntyped(jsonString);

System.debug(result.get('name'));
Serialize Apex → JSON
Map<String, Object> data = new Map<String, Object>();
data.put('name', 'Raj');

String jsonOutput = JSON.serialize(data);
Use Cases
REST API communication
External system data handling
