Integration Limits (Very Important for Interviews)

Salesforce enforces strict governor limits to maintain multi-tenant stability.

Key Callout Limits
Max callouts per transaction: 100
Timeout limit: 120 seconds (default shorter if not set)
Heap size:
Sync: ~6 MB
Async: ~12 MB
Critical Constraints
Callouts not allowed after DML (unless async)
Triggers cannot directly make callouts → use:
@future(callout=true)
Queueable Apex
Example (Async Callout)
@future(callout=true)
public static void sendData() {
    Http http = new Http();
    HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
    req.setEndpoint('https://api.example.com');
    req.setMethod('POST');
    http.send(req);
}
2. Middleware (Enterprise Integration Layer)

Instead of directly connecting Salesforce to every system, companies use middleware.

Popular Tools
MuleSoft (Salesforce-owned)
Dell Boomi
Informatica
Why Middleware?
Without Middleware

Salesforce ↔ System A
Salesforce ↔ System B
Salesforce ↔ System C

→ Tightly coupled, hard to maintain

With Middleware

Salesforce → Middleware → Multiple Systems

→ Loose coupling + scalability

Responsibilities of Middleware
Data transformation (JSON ↔ XML)
Authentication handling
Retry mechanisms
Error logging
Rate limiting
